ix involved explicitly converting all GDP per capita columns to float64 before calculating skewness.\n\nKey observations:\n1. The CSV file contained GDP per capita values as strings, causing scipy.stats.skew to fail with "ValueError: data type <class \'numpy.object_\'> not inexact"\n2. Solution: Convert all gdpPercap_* columns to float64 using df[col].astype(float)\n3. After conversion, successfully calculated skewness for each country across all years\n4. Myanmar was identified as the country with the highest skewness (2.6056)\n\nThe final answer is: @highest_skewness_country[Myanmar]\n```\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom scipy.stats import skew\n\n# Load the dataset\ndf = pd.read_csv(\'./input/gapminder_gdp_asia.csv\')\n\n# Convert GDP per capita columns to numeric types\ngdp_columns = [col for col in df.columns if col.startswith(\'gdpPercap_\')]\nfor col in gdp_columns:\n    df[col] = df[col].astype(float)\n\n# Calculate skewness for each country across all years\nskewness_by_country = {}\nfor _, row in df.iterrows():\n    country = row[\'country\']\n    gdp_values = row[gdp_columns].values.astype(float)\n    if len(gdp_values) > 2:\n        skewness = skew(gdp_values, bias=False)\n        skewness_by_country[country] = skewness\n\n# Create dataframe and find country with highest skewness\nskewness_df = pd.DataFrame(list(skewness_by_country.items()), columns=[\'country\', \'skewness\'])\nskewness_df = skewness_df.sort_values(\'skewness\', ascending=False)\nhighest_skewness_country = skewness_df.iloc[0][\'country\']\n\nprint(f"Country with highest skewness: {highest_skewness_country}")\nprint(f"Skewness value: {skewness_df.iloc[0][\'skewness\']:.4f}")\n```\n```markdown\n{\n  "final_answer_block": {\n    "question_id": 252,\n    "answers": {"highest_skewness_country": "Myanmar"},\n    "format_targets": {"highest_skewness_country": "@highest_skewness_country[Myanmar]"},\n    "evidence": {\n      "source_columns": ["country", "gdpPercap_1952", "gdpPercap_1957", 